[Music]  [Music]  PlayStation VR always had a tough road  ahead of it very high-end gaming pcs  needed to power the oculus rift in HTC  vive the PlayStation 4 is underpowered  the fact that Sony was retrofitting it  sold 2010 released move controllers and  PlayStation camera to work with psvr  which were peripherals that weren't  initially designed for vo are also does  not bode well for the platform after  going eyes on with it for the past few  days and must admit that it surprisingly  impressed it in some areas but it also  has some issues one area where psvr  could use improvement is with resolution  the visuals unfortunately look a little  blurry the head-mounted display uses a  5.7 inch 1920 by 1080 resolution  stereoscopic 3d screen while 1080p is  fine on a TV that's several feet in  front of you it's a whole different  story when the display is magnified in  front of your eyes PSV are also supports  a hundred twenty Hertz refresh rate  panel which allows the hmd to render up  to 120 frames per second having a high  frame rate is essential in VR because  when the frame rate dips too low VR  nausea may start to kick in poor head  tracking can also lead to view our  motion sickness luckily that isn't a  problem here PS VR has nine positional  tracking LEDs on the hmd and features  just 18 milliseconds of latency the  headset itself may look bulky but it  feels really light despite wing 1.3  pounds it helps that the weight of psvr  is bounce from the front to the back its  overall Organon makes it the most  comfortable HMV to wear psvr supplies  its own earbuds and supports 3d audio  which coupled with positional head  tracking does a great job enhancing the  immersion of the arm it is important to  note that psvr requires a PlayStation  camera which is sold separately from the  base package furthermore to get the full  VR experience and to get your hands into  VR you're gonna want to get Sony's Move  controllers which are also sold  separately  psvr can deliver some incredibly  immersive experiences aspiring a passive  experience a shark encounter which is a  short demo within the PlayStation VR  worlds compilation can be riveting it  virtually places you in a shark cage and  anchors you deep down into the ocean  when you encounter a massive great white  shark that's out to eat you seeing it  pry open your cage with its sharp teeth  provides a sense of danger that is  impossible to replicate on a traditional  display the London heist is also another  psvr highlight seeing a mobster point  the barrel of his gun at your head is  unnerving but in the coolest way being  able to shoot back at gangsters is also  wildly fun  one concern that I had going into this  review is whether or not the move  controllers would be accurate enough for  VR but they do a surprisingly good job  of tracking your hands while there's a  lot to like about psvr it also has  issues the PlayStation camera has a  relatively small field of view and  you'll lose tracking if either your  controller or headset pops outside of  its fov also when I stepped more than  eight feet away from the camera  everything started to awkwardly shift in  and out it's not the end of the world by  any means but it definitely feels like  an ecosystem that's cobbled together  using existing parts furthermore because  psvr only uses one camera the move  controllers will get included if you  turn completely around as the camera  needs to see the light emitting from the  controller to track it what this means  on a fundamental level is that we are  likely not to get 360 experiences it's  also unfortunate that the Move  controllers don't have a d-pad or  movement stick as this further limits  PSP RS capabilities  despite all these little technical  deficiencies the biggest issue plaguing  psvr is motion sickness your mileage may  vary here as some people don't have  issues with dr nausea i unfortunately  i'm not one of them many of the  first-person launch games made me feel  ill the main culprit here is artificial  locomotion this is when a game binds  first-person movement to a joystick  while this is fine on a flat-screen TV  in VR your eyes are tricked into  thinking your body is moving but your  body does not get the same cues this  disconnect can lead to wobbly sea legs  sony tells me that gamers should take  15-minute breaks each hour to combat  motion sickness but that's not a very  good long-term solution while PSP r is  cheaper than either the rift or the vive  and doesn't require an expensive pc  there is a reason that high-quality VR  is expensive sure PS VR is more  affordable but it also takes some  shortcuts while it sometimes works  surprisingly well given its constraints  it reminds me of the adage you get what  you pay for as the VR fan I want psvr to  succeed but it trips up a bit too much  too wholeheartedly recommend right now  if you have a ps4 and are dying to get  pious VR then I'd recommend the $500  bundle which includes the PlayStation  camera to move controllers and  PlayStation VR worlds if you have a ps4  and are simply curious about the R I'd  hold off until more titles are released  for everyone else I'd recommend saving  up for a better reality for more gaming  and hardware coverage stay tuned to gain  spot calm and subscribe to our YouTube  channel  [Music]

